<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Max Weber</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Maximilian Carl Emil Weber</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Max Weber was a German sociologist, historian, jurist and political economist, one of the central figures in the development of sociology. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> in 1864, he taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, Freiburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, and in The Protestant Ethic and the Spirit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">Capitalism</a> argued that the Protestant work ethic influenced the creation of modern capitalism. He defined states by their monopoly on the legitimate use of force within a given area and divided social authority into charismatic, traditional and rational-legal forms. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he co-founded the German Democratic Party and advised the drafting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-constitution">Weimar Constitution</a>; he died in 1920, leaving Economy and Society unfinished.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/max-weber.jpg" alt="Max Weber" width="330" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ernst Gottmann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Max_Weber,_1918.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Maximilian Carl Emil Weber was born on 21 April 1864 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> and raised in Charlottenburg, the oldest of eight children. His father, Max Weber Sr., was a lawyer, civil servant and National Liberal parliamentarian, and the family's salon welcomed scholars such as the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-mommsen">Theodor Mommsen</a>. His mother, Helene Fallenstein, was a devout Calvinist and philanthropist. He studied law and history in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, took his doctorate in law in 1889 and his habilitation in 1891, and in 1893 married his second cousin Marianne Schnitger, a feminist activist and author.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Weber taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, Freiburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>. In 1897 he suffered a breakdown after his father died following an argument, and he ceased teaching and travelled for several years. From 1904 he was an associate editor of the Archiv für Sozialwissenschaft und Sozialpolitik, in which he published The Protestant Ethic and the Spirit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">Capitalism</a>.</p><p>He first supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s war effort in 1914 but became a prominent critic of expansionism and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a>'s war policies, and he called for democratisation. After the war he co-founded the German Democratic Party, advised on the drafting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-constitution">Weimar Constitution</a>, and taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, where he gave the lectures Science as a Vocation and Politics as a Vocation. He died of pneumonia on 14 June 1920, possibly as a result of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-flu">Spanish flu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>A central concern was rationalisation and disenchantment. In The Protestant Ethic he argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>, tied piety to a worldly calling and so encouraged rational economic gain. He later studied the religions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. He defined the state by its monopoly on the legitimate use of force within a given area, and divided legitimate authority into traditional, charismatic and rational-legal forms.</p><p>Methodologically he placed social action at the centre of sociology, sought to understand it through interpretation, and used ideal types as models to compare with reality. Economy and Society was left unfinished. He is now regarded alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emile-durkheim">Émile Durkheim</a> as a founding figure of sociology.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Max Weber” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Max Weber</b>’s essay The Protestant Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism (1904–1905) linked Protestant, especially Calvinist, discipline to the rise of capitalism, a thesis still debated and widely criticised.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Robert K. Merton argued for a link between Puritanism and early experimental science, a thesis still debated, and <b>Max Weber</b> tied Calvinist self-denial to the rise of capitalism.</blockquote><a href="/movement/puritans">Puritans · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Max Weber</b>’s essay The City (1921) treated the self-governing commune of citizens as peculiar to the West; the Copenhagen comparisons, which found city-state cultures from Mesopotamia to West Africa and Mesoamerica, show that the form arose independently in several parts of the world.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Max Weber</b> saw the plebiscite as a tool of Caesarist leaders, as when Napoleon and Napoleon III had their seizures of power approved by popular vote.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Conservatives tend to believe in traditional authority, which <b>Max Weber</b> described as resting on “the sanctity of immemorial traditions”, and in hierarchy.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>British Marxist historians such as Christopher Hill, E. P. Thompson and Eric Hobsbawm developed history from below, and the Frankfurt School combined some Marxist concepts with the ideas of <b>Max Weber</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/marxism">Marxism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.98,11.03&amp;year=1900" aria-label="Erfurt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Max Weber"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5590 5719h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5590 5719h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.98,11.03&amp;year=1900">What was here in 1900? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
